    "postMarkdown": "When Agent A suggests a trade, Agent B evaluates credit risk, and Agent C executes the order in the market—who is accountable if something goes wrong?\n\nMulti-agent systems break traditional single-actor governance models. When decisions are emergent and distributed, accountability must be re-engineered.\n\n1. 1. Distributed Blame & Causality: Tracking the exact conversational trace and parameter state across every agent involved in a multi-step transaction.\n\n2. 2. Agent Tool Permission Creep: Ensuring that combining multiple safe agents does not create an unsafe composite capability that bypasses corporate policy.\n\n3. 3. Sovereign Memory Governance: Managing what proprietary context is retained in episodic memory and ensuring compliance with right-to-be-forgotten laws.\n\n4. 4. Synthetic Whistleblowing: Deploying independent watchdog agents that monitor internal agent communication channels for policy violations or collusion.\n\nAgentic governance requires governing the interactions between agents, not just the prompts fed into them.\n\n---\n→ Request an Agentic Governance Assessment with TrustGrid: https://trustgrid.ai\n\n#AgenticGovernance #MultiAgentSystems #EnterpriseRisk #Compliance #TrustGrid\n",
